=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dswa1, dswa2
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** nftables FORWARD chain on both Office A distribution switches (dswa1 and dswa2), operating on the VLAN 10 bridged interface `br0.10`.

**Mechanism:** Both dswa1 and dswa2 carry an identical two-rule nftables policy in their FORWARD chain. The first rule explicitly accepts ICMP (ping) from 10.1.0.0/24 (Office A) to 10.3.0.0/24 (Office B). The second rule — with no protocol qualifier — drops all remaining IP traffic between the same source and destination subnets. Because nftables rules are evaluated top-to-bottom and stop at the first match, any TCP or UDP packet from Office A destined for Office B matches only the drop rule. The TCP SYN to srv1 is silently discarded at the distribution layer, resulting in a connection timeout rather than a TCP RST.

**Localization:** The fault is on dswa1 (FORWARD chain, `br0.10` interface) and dswa2 (FORWARD chain, `br0.10` interface). Both devices carry the same rule pair, so traffic is blocked regardless of which distribution switch the ECMP or spanning-tree path selects. ICMP counters on dswa1 (4 packets accepted) confirm active ICMP traffic while TCP counters on the drop rule remain at zero because no TCP was attempted during this diagnostic window.

**Root cause:** An ACL policy added last night on dswa1 and dswa2 blocks all non-ICMP traffic originating from the Office A subnet (10.1.0.0/24) toward the Office B subnet (10.3.0.0/24).

**Fix:** Delete the drop rule from the FORWARD chain on both dswa1 and dswa2 — `nft delete rule ip filter FORWARD handle <handle>` for the generic drop entry — and, if inter-campus connectivity should be unrestricted, remove or rewrite the entire two-rule block. The ICMP accept rule above it may also be removed once the drop is gone, as the default chain policy is `accept`.
=== END ===